  "id": "COMPOUND:19492 - PROTEIN:6273",
  "verbalization": "Radicinol appears to interact with DNA polymerase nu, which is a key enzyme in DNA replication, and this interaction is mediated by two distinct compounds: 6-O-[4-O-(6-O-Syringyloyl-Beta-D-Glucopyranosyl)Vanilloyl]-Beta-D-Glucopyranosyl 4-Hydroxy-3,5-Dimethoxybenzoate and Sibiskoside. Both compounds are known to target DNA polymerase nu, and their shared targeting of this enzyme suggests a common mechanism of action. The third compound, [7-(4-hydroxyphenyl)-4-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxy-3-(3,4,5-trihydroxyphenyl)-2,8-dioxatricyclo[7.3.1.05,13]trideca-1(12),3,5(13),6,9-pentaen-11-ylidene]oxidanium, also targets DNA polymerase nu, further reinforcing the notion that Radicinol's interaction with this enzyme is mediated by multiple pathways. The presence of multiple targets for DNA polymerase nu indicates that Radicinol may influence DNA replication through various mechanisms, potentially affecting the cell's ability to replicate its genetic material. This suggests that Radicinol may have a role in modulating DNA polymerase activity, which could have implications for cellular processes involving DNA synthesis and repair.",
